// Atlas relay tool policy: the agent may use ONLY the four Atlas MCP tools.
//
// A strict allow-list removes every built-in tool (web_search, web_fetch,
// exec, process/shell, memory_search, browser). That keeps the small local
// qwen3:8b model on rails AND enforces the security boundary — no shell, no
// web, no file access — for an agent reachable from Telegram.
//
// MCP tools are namespaced "<server>__<tool>".
//
// Scope note: atlas_ask is read-only. The other three have side effects —
// atlas_research spawns a background run that writes into the vault,
// atlas_sync_vault commits and pushes it. They are safe to expose here
// because capabilities.py launches only names in its fixed registry, always
// with an argument list and never through a shell, and because the Telegram
// listener only accepts messages from TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_ID. The blast radius
// of a hostile message is therefore "a research run I did not ask for",
// not code execution.
//
// Applied with: openclaw config patch --file openclaw/tools-policy.patch.json5
{
  tools: {
    allow: [
      "atlas__atlas_ask",
      "atlas__atlas_research",
      "atlas__atlas_capability_status",
      "atlas__atlas_sync_vault"
    ],
    deny: ["web_search", "web_fetch", "exec", "process", "browser"]
  }
}
